2b:["$","section",null,{"className":"py-16 md:py-20 px-4","children":["$","div",null,{"className":"max-w-5xl mx-auto","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"text-center max-w-3xl mx-auto mb-10","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"text-teal-600 font-bold uppercase tracking-wider text-sm","children":"Frequently Asked Questions"}],["$","h2",null,{"className":"mt-3 text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-slate-900","children":"Paediatric ENT FAQs"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"mt-4 text-slate-600 text-lg","children":"Common questions parents ask about children's ear, nose, throat, hearing, breathing and sleep-related problems."}]]}],["$","$L7d",null,{"faqItems":[{"question":"What is a paediatric ENT specialist?","answer":"A paediatric ENT specialist evaluates and treats ear, nose and throat conditions in children, including ear infections, hearing problems, enlarged adenoids and tonsils, nasal obstruction, allergies, snoring and sleep-related breathing problems."},{"question":"When should my child see an ENT doctor?","answer":"Consider an ENT evaluation when a child has recurrent ear infections, persistent hearing concerns, speech or language concerns, chronic nasal blockage, habitual mouth breathing, frequent snoring, recurrent tonsillitis, persistent ear discharge or other recurring ENT symptoms."},{"question":"What are signs of hearing loss in a child?","answer":"Possible signs include not responding consistently to sounds or their name, frequently asking for repetition, increasing television volume, difficulty following conversations, unclear speech or concerns about speech and language development. A hearing assessment can help determine whether hearing is contributing."},{"question":"Can ear infections affect a child's hearing?","answer":"Yes. Middle-ear fluid or inflammation can temporarily reduce hearing. Recurrent or persistent ear problems should be assessed, particularly when parents notice changes in communication, attention or speech."},{"question":"Can enlarged adenoids cause mouth breathing?","answer":"Enlarged adenoids can contribute to nasal obstruction and mouth breathing. Other causes are also possible, so persistent mouth breathing should be evaluated rather than assuming that adenoids are always responsible."},{"question":"Is snoring normal in children?","answer":"Occasional snoring can occur during a cold or nasal congestion. Frequent or loud snoring, particularly when associated with restless sleep, breathing pauses or daytime concerns, deserves medical evaluation."},{"question":"When are tonsils or adenoids removed in children?","answer":"Surgery may be considered when enlarged tonsils or adenoids cause significant or persistent problems such as sleep-disordered breathing, recurrent infections or other clinically important symptoms. The decision depends on the child's individual assessment."},{"question":"Does every child with an ear infection need antibiotics?","answer":"No. Treatment depends on the child's age, symptoms, examination findings and whether the condition is likely to be viral, bacterial or related to another cause. Antibiotics should be used when clinically appropriate rather than automatically."},{"question":"Can glue ear affect speech and learning?","answer":"Persistent middle-ear fluid can reduce hearing. If hearing is significantly affected for a prolonged period, it may interfere with communication and learning. Children with suspected persistent hearing difficulty should be assessed."},{"question":"Does my child need a hearing test?","answer":"A hearing assessment may be recommended when there are concerns about hearing, speech or language development, recurrent middle-ear problems, poor response to sound or other risk factors. The appropriate test depends on the child's age and ability to participate."},{"question":"When should childhood snoring be considered a concern?","answer":"Frequent or loud snoring, mouth breathing, restless sleep, unusual sleeping positions, witnessed breathing pauses or daytime behavioural and concentration concerns should prompt an assessment for possible sleep-disordered breathing."},{"question":"Where can I consult a paediatric ENT specialist in Delhi?","answer":"Dr.(Prof.) Dhirendra Singh Kushwah provides ENT consultation in South Delhi, including clinic locations in C.R. Park and Arjun Nagar, Safdarjung Enclave. Parents can contact the clinic for an appointment and discuss the child's symptoms before the visit."}]}]]}]}]
